<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Keir Hardie</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1856">1856</a>–<a href="/years/1915">1915</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as James Keir Hardie</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Keir Hardie was a Scottish trade unionist and politician, a founder of the Labour Party and its first parliamentary leader, from 1906 to 1908. A miner in Lanarkshire from the age of ten, he became a union organiser in 1879 and in 1886 secretary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayrshire">Ayrshire</a> Miners' Union. Elected for West Ham South as an independent in 1892, he helped to form the Independent Labour Party in 1893 and in 1900 the Labour Representation Committee, which became the Labour Party. He later campaigned for women's suffrage and against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/keir-hardie.jpg" alt="Keir Hardie" width="320" height="460" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Grantham Bain / Adam Cuerden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Keir_Hardie_by_George_Grantham_Bain.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
